[comment {-*- tcl -*- doctools manpage}]
[manpage_begin "Event log" n ""]
[copyright "2026, Ashok P. Nadkarni"]
[moddesc "Event Log"]
[titledesc "Commands relating to reading and writing the Windows event log"]
[require twapi_evt]
[description]
[para]

The [cmd twapi_evt] package implements commands for reading and writing to
the Windows Event log.

[section Sessions]

An event log (EVT) [emph session] is a connection to the event log service on
a system. Access to event log channels and event publishers takes place through
sessions. TclOO objects of class [uri #evtsessioncreate "EvtSession"] wrap EVT
sessions and provide factory methods for creating objects representing publishers,
channels etc.
The command below will create a [cmd EvtSession] object [cmd sess] for the
local event log using the default credentials of the current thread.

[example_begin]
EvtSession create sess
[example_end]

Options allow connection to remote systems and different credentials.

[para]
Session objects must be destroyed when no longer needed.

[example_begin]
sess destroy
[example_end]

This will free the underlying session handles as well as destroy any TclOO
objects created using its factory methods.

[section "Channels and file stores"]

Event log [emph channels] are named event streams storing event records
that can be read and written. The configuration properties for a channel are
managed through objects of the [uri #evtchannelconfig [cmd EvtChannelConfig]]
class created through the [cmd EvtSession]
[uri #createchannelconfig [cmd "createChannelConfig"]] or
[uri #newchannelconfig [cmd "newChannelConfig"]] methods.
The channels registered on a system can be retrieved with the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #registeredchannels [cmd "registeredChannels"]] method.

[para]
Event records can also be stored in files by exporting them from a channel
with the [uri #exportevents [cmd exportEvents]] method. These can be read
back from the file using the same mechanisms as used for channels. Events
exported from a channel do not store message strings associated with the
events. To include these, the file must be further processed using the
[uri #archivelogfile [cmd archiveLogFile]] method.

[para]
Information about individual channels and file stores can be retrieved using
objects of class [uri #evtloginfo [cmd EvtLogInfo]] created by the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createloginfo [cmd "createLogInfo"]] or
[uri #newloginfo [cmd "newLogInfo"]] methods.

[section "Reading events"]

Events are read from channels and file stores by invoking
the [uri #getevents [cmd getEvents]] method on objects of class
[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]] created using
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createreader [cmd "createReader"]] or
[uri #newreader [cmd "newReader"]] methods. The event handles
returned are passed to rendering functions depending on the
event data desired.

[section "Rendering events"]

There are several options for retrieving the event data referenced
by event handles returned from [uri #getevents [cmd getEvents]].
The methods of the [uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]] class
provides high-level access to event data. Objects of this class are
created by the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createformatter [cmd "createFormatter"]] or
[uri #newformatter [cmd "newFormatter"]] methods. The class takes
care of mapping raw event data to its publisher's metadata for
localized messages, levels, tasks etc.
[para]
Alternatively, the following lower level functions may be used. These
leave the caller responsible for retrieving associated publisher
metadata if needed.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_logpath [cmd evt_event_logpath]]]
Returns the channel or file from which the event was read.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]]]
Returns event fields as a list based on a rendering context.
[opt_def [uri #evt_event_xml [cmd evt_event_xml]]]
Returns the event fields in XML format. Does not include rendered
messages. Use the [cmd EvtFormatter]
[uri #formateventasxml [cmd formatEventAsXml]] if that is
required.
[list_end]

The [cmd evt_event_render] command requires a
[emph "render context"] to be passed which selects the event properties
that are to be rendered from an event. The following commands create
a render context.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_system [cmd evt_render_context_system]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for system-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_userdata [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for publisher-defined fields.
[opt_def [uri #evt_render_context_xpaths [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths]]]
Returns a handle to a render context for fields matching
XPATH expressions.
[list_end]

[section Publishers]
An event [emph publisher], or [emph provider] (the Windows SDK uses the term
interchangeably) defines the structure of events it may generate, the channels
to which it writes events, and localized message strings. This structure is
described by metadata in a publisher manifest that is installed on the system
or in [uri #archivelogfile "archived event files"] so it can be accessed without
registration on the system.
The list of publishers registered on a system can be retrieved with the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #publishers [cmd "publishers"]] method.

[para]
The [uri #evtpublisher [cmd EvtPublisher]] class encapsulates publisher metadata.
Objects of this class are created by the
[cmd EvtSession] [uri #createpublisher [cmd "createPublisher"]] or
[uri #newpublisher [cmd "newPublisher"]] methods. [cmd EvtPublisher] methods
can be then be used to retrieve various metadata for the publisher. For example,
the following prints the channels defined by Hyper-V.

[example_begin]
% sess createPublisher pub Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute
% foreach chan [lb]pub channels] {puts [lb]dict get $chan -channelpath]}
Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute-Admin
Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute-Operational
Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute-Analytic
[example_end]

Or to get the events defined by the publisher and the corresponding
localized message string in French (LCID 1036),

[example_begin]
% sess createPublisher frpub Microsoft-Windows-Hyper-V-Compute -lcid 1036
::frpub
% foreach event_def [lb]frpub events {-eventid -messageid}] {
    puts [lb]dict get $event_def -eventid]:[lb]frpub message [lb]dict get $event_def -messageid]]
}
...
500:Started DM operation add memory. Balancer requested to add %2 pages. (Virtual machine ID %1)
501:DM operation add memory completed %3 pages were added, time %2 s. (Virtual machine ID %1)
502:Started DM operation remove memory. Balancer requested to remove %2 pages. (Virtual machine ID %1)
...
1000:Le service de calcul hôte démarre.
1001:Service de calcul hôte démarré.
1002:Le service de calcul hôte est en cours d’arrêt.
1003:Service de calcul hôte arrêté.
...
[example_end]

Note above that localized messages depend on the appropriate locale being installed
and the publisher metadata including a localized string for the message id bound
to the event.

[section Commands]

[list_begin definitions]

[call [cmd evt_event_logpath] [arg HEVT]]
Returns the path of the channel or file from which the event
identified by [arg HEVT] was retrieved.

[call [cmd evt_event_render] [arg HEVENT] [arg HCTX]]
Returns a list of field values from event referenced by [arg HEVENT]
as per the rendering context referenced by [arg HCTX] which must be
a handle returned by
[uri #evt_render_context_xpaths [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths]],
[uri #evt_render_context_userdata [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]], or
[uri #evt_render_context_system [cmd evt_render_context_system]].
The order of values in the first two cases is the order of the XPATHS
in the render context and order of event's user data fields respectively. In
the third case, the order is always that of the
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_system_property_id "EVT_SYSTEM_PROPERTY_ID"]
enumeration in the Windows SDK.

[call [cmd evt_event_xml] [arg HEVENT]]

Returns the XML representation of the event referenced by [arg HEVENT],
including including system-defined metadata and provider-defined event data.

[call [cmd evt_render_context_system]]
Returns a handle to a render context that can be passed to
[uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]] to render the
system-defined properties of an event.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]]
when no longer needed.

[call [cmd evt_render_context_userdata]]
Returns a handle to a render context that can be passed to
[uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]] to render the
user-defined properties of an event under the [const UserData]
section of the event, and the [const EventData] section if no
[const UserData] section is present.
[nl]
The returned values correspond to the properties defined by the
publisher in the event schema and returned in the same order.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]]
when no longer needed.

[call [cmd evt_render_context_xpaths] [arg XPATHLIST]]

Returns a handle to a render context for rendering the event properties
selected by the list of XPATH expressions passed in [arg XPATHLIST]
which must contain at least one XPATH expression.
The handle can then be passed to
[uri #evt_event_render [cmd evt_event_render]] to render the selected
property values.
[nl]
Each element of [arg XPATHLIST] specifies an XPath expression that
selects a [emph single] property from an event, for example
[const Event/System/Provider/@Name] or [const Event/System/Channel].
The values are returned in the
same order as the corresponding XPath expressions in the list.
[nl]
The returned handle should be released using [cmd evt_close].

[call [cmd EvtFormatter]]
Class for rendering and formatting events.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createformatter [cmd createFormatter]] or
[uri #newformatter [cmd newFormatter]] and not directly.

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd decodeEvent]" [arg HEVT] [arg PROPERTIES]]

Returns a dictionary containing event data in fully decoded form.
[arg HEVT] is a handle to a single event. See the
[uri #decodeevents [cmd decodeEvents]] method for more information.
This method only differs in that it accepts a single handle and
return format is a dictionary.

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd decodeEvents]" [arg HEVTS] [arg PROPERTIES]]

Returns a [uri base.html#recordarrays "record array"] containing
event data in fully decoded form. [arg HEVTS] is a list of handles
to events as returned from [uri #getEvents [cmd getEvents]].
The fields of the record array are those specified in [arg PROPERTIES]
which defaults to the list [const -providername], [const -eventid],
[const -level], [const -timecreated] and [const -message].
The table below shows the possible event fields that may be included
in [arg PROPERTIES].
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -activityid]]
GUID containing the activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -channel]]
The channel to which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -computer]]
The name of the system on which the event was logged.
[opt_def [cmd -eventid]]
The event id for the event's definition in the publisher's metadata.
[opt_def [cmd -eventrecordid]]
The id of this event record.
[opt_def [cmd -keywords]]
Contains a list of keywords with which the event is tagged.
[opt_def [cmd -level]]
The localized string associated by the publisher with the numeric level.
[opt_def [cmd -message]]
Contains the localized formatted message corresponding to the event. This
is similar to what is displayed in the Windows Event Viewer application.
[opt_def [cmd -opcode]]
The localized string associated by the publisher with the event opcode.
[opt_def [cmd -pid]]
The process id of the process that logged the event.
[opt_def [cmd -providerguid]]
The GUID of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -providername]]
The name of the logging event provider.
[opt_def [cmd -qualifiers]]
The high 16 bits of the event id for legacy event providers. This is combined with
the [cmd -eventid] field as low 16 bits to form a 32-bit id to retrieve messages.
[opt_def [cmd -relatedactivityid]]
GUID containing the relative activity id associated with the event.
[opt_def [cmd -task]]
The localized string associated by the publisher with the event task property.
[opt_def [cmd -tid]]
The thread id of the logging thread.
[opt_def [cmd -timecreated]]
The time the event was logged in 100ns units since January 1, 1601 UTC.
[opt_def [cmd -sid]]
The SID of the account under which the logging thread was running.
[opt_def [cmd -userdata]]
A list of the publisher-specific properties for the event.
[opt_def [cmd -version]]
The version of the event definition.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd formatEvent]" [arg HEVT]]
Returns the localized formatted message form of an event.
[arg HEVT] is a handle to an event.

[call "[arg EVTFMTOBJ] [cmd formatEventAsXml]" [arg HEVT]]
Returns event data in XML format. [arg HEVT] is a handle to an event.
Unlike the [uri #evteventxml [cmd evt_event_xml]] command, this includes
a [const <RenderingInfo>] XML element with the localized strings for the event.

[call [cmd EvtChannelConfig]]
Class wrapping a handle to a channel configuration information.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createchannelconfig [cmd createChannelConfig]] or
[uri #newchannelconfig [cmd newChannelConfig]] and not directly.

[call [cmd EvtPublisher]]
Class wrapping a handle to the metadata of an event publisher.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createPublisher [cmd createPublisher]] or
[uri #newpublisher [cmd newPublisher]] and not directly.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd channels]"]
Returns a list of the publisher's channel definitions. Each element is
a dictionary with the following keys:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channelid]]
The numeric id assigned to the channel as per its value attribute.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceID]
[opt_def [cmd -channelindex]]
The publisher assigned channel identifier.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceIndex].
[opt_def [cmd -channelflags]]
[const 1] if the channel was imported from another publisher's manifest.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceFlags]
[opt_def [cmd -channelmessageid]]
Contains the numeric message id for the channel name.
This can be passed
to the [uri #message [cmd message]] method to retrieve the name of the
channel, generally the last component of the full channel path.
[const -1] if not defined.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferenceMessageID]
[opt_def [cmd -channelpath]]
Contains the canonical full path of the channel, e.g.
[const Microsoft-Windows-EventLog/Debug].
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataChannelReferencePath].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd events]" [arg PROPNAMES]]
Returns a list of the publisher's event definitions.
Each event's definition
includes the event's metadata properties as a dictionary keyed by
the event property names passed in [arg PROPNAMES].
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -channel]
The numeric value attribute of the associated channel. 0 if no association.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventChannel].
[opt_def -eventid]
The numeric value attribute of the event definition which is its id when
the event is logged.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventID].
[opt_def -keywords]
64-bit keyword mask of the all keywords for the event definition.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventKeyword].
[opt_def -level]
The level attribute of the event definition. 0 if not specified.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventLevel].
[opt_def -messageid]
Message id attribute that can be passed to the
[uri #message [cmd message]] method to retrieve the localized string
for the event. [const -1] if unassigned.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventMessageID].
[opt_def -opcode]
The opcode attribute of the event. 0 if not specified.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventOpcode].
[opt_def -task]
The task attribute of the event. 0 if not specified.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventTask].
[opt_def -template]
The XML string from the template attribute of the event definition.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventTemplate].
[opt_def -version]
The version attribute of the event definition.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_event_metadata_property_id EventMetadataEventVersion].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd guid]"]
Returns the GUID of the publisher whose metadata is encapsulated by [arg EVTPUBOBJ].

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd handle]"]
Returns the EVT handle of the publisher metadata encapsulated by [arg EVTPUBOBJ].

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd helpLink]"]
Returns the URL of the publisher's help page.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd keywordLabels]"]
Returns a dictionary mapping keyword decimal values to localized labels.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd keywords]"]
Returns a list of the publisher's keyword definitions. Each element is
a dictionary with the following keys.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -messageid]
Message id attribute of the keyword that can be passed to the
[uri #message [cmd message]] method to retrieve the localized string
for the keyword. [const -1] if undefined.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataKeywordMessageID].
[opt_def -name]
Name attribute of the keyword.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataKeywordName].
[opt_def -value]
Value attribute of the keyword.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataKeywordValue].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd lcid]"]
The locale id that will be used to access the publisher metadata
encapsulated by [arg EVTPUBLOBJ].

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd levelLabels]"]
Returns a dictionary mapping event level decimal values to localized labels.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd levels]"]
Returns a list of the publisher's event level definitions. Each element is
a dictionary with the following keys:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -messageid]
Message id attribute that can be passed to the
[uri #message [cmd message]] method to retrieve the localized string
for the level. [const -1] if undefined.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataMessageID].
[opt_def -name]
Name attribute of the level.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataLevelName].
[opt_def -value]
Value attribute of the level.
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataLevelValue].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd message]" [arg MSGID]]
Returns the localized string for message id [arg MSGID].

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd messageFile]"]
Returns the path to the publisher's message file.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd messageId]"]
Message id attribute that can be passed to the
[uri #message [cmd message]] method to retrieve the localized string
for the publisher name. [const -1] if undefined.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd name]"]
Returns the name of the publisher whose metadata is wrapped by [arg EVTPUBOBJ].

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd opcodeNames]"]
Returns a dictionary mapping opcode decimal values to localized labels.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd opcodes]"]
Returns a list of the publisher's opcode definitions. Each element is
a dictionary with the following keys:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -messageid]
Message id attribute that can be passed to the
[uri #message [cmd message]] method to retrieve the localized string
for the opcode. [const -1] if undefined.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataOpcodeMessageID].
[opt_def -name]
Name attribute of the opcode.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataOpcodeName].
[opt_def -value]
Value attribute of the opcode.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataOpcodeValue].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd parameterFile]"]
Returns the path to the publisher's parameter file.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd resourceFile]"]
Returns the path to the publisher's resource file.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd taskNames]"]
Returns a dictionary mapping task decimal values to localized labels.

[call "[arg EVTPUBOBJ] [cmd tasks]"]
Returns a list of the publisher's opcode definitions. Each element is
a dictionary with the following keys:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -eventguid]
The eventGuid attribute of the task.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataTaskEventGuid].
[opt_def -messageid]
Message id attribute that can be passed to the
[uri #message [cmd message]] method to retrieve the localized string
for the task. [const -1] if undefined.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataTaskMessageID].
[opt_def -name]
Name attribute of the task.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataTaskName]
[opt_def -value]
Value attribute of the task.
See [uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_publisher_metadata_property_id EvtPublisherMetadataTaskValue].
[list_end]

[call [cmd EvtReader]]

[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]] class instances can be used to read
events from a result set returned by a query on channels or event log files.
To guard against future implementation changes, objects of
this class should be created through
[uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd EvtSession]] methods
[uri #createreader [cmd createReader]] or
[uri #newreader [cmd newReader]] and not directly.

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd getEvents]" [opt [arg options]]]
Returns a list containing events from an event result set.
The returned list elements are event handles which can be rendered
using [uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]] or commands
[uri #evteventxml [cmd evt_event_xml]] and
[uri #evteventrender [cmd evt_event_render]].
The event handles
must be explicitly closed via [uri #evt_close [cmd evt_close]].
They are not automatically closed along with the session.
[nl]
The command returns an empty list if no more events are available
in the result set. Additional events logged to the channel subsequent
to the query are not returned.
[nl]
The cursor in the event result set is moved past the returned events
so each [cmd getEvents] call returns events from the result set in sequence.
[nl]
The following options can be specified:
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -count] [arg COUNT]]
Returns [arg COUNT] events from the result set. The actual number of
events returned may be less if the end of the result set is reached.
If unspecified, the number of returned events depends on various
internal tuning factors and applications must not rely on a specific
number being returned.
[opt_def [cmd -statusvar] [arg VARNAME]]
The name of a variable in which to return status. If there is an
error when reading the next event, the command will normally generate
an Tcl exception. If this option is specified, the command will instead
return an empty list and store the error code in the specified variable.
[opt_def [cmd -timeout] [arg MILLISECONDS]]
Specifies the number of milliseconds to wait for events. A value of
[const -1] (default) indicates call should not time out.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd handle]"]
Returns the handle to the underlying EVT query.

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd info]"]
Returns the status information for a query. For a structured XML
query, this is a list each element of which is a pair consisting of
a channel name in the query and a Win32 error code.

[call "[arg EVTREADEROBJ] [cmd seek]" [arg OFFSET] [opt [arg options]]]

Changes the position of the cursor from which events are read in the
wrapped result set by [arg OFFSET] records. A negative value will move
the cursor in the backward direction. A positive value will move it
forward. By default the offset is interpreted relative to the current
position of the cursor. The following options may be passed to the
method.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -hbookmark] [arg HBOOK]]
Causes [arg OFFSET] to be interpreted relative to the bookmark referenced
by the handle [arg HBOOK].
[opt_def [cmd -origin] [arg ORIGIN]]
Causes [arg OFFSET] to be interpreted relative to the current cursor
position, the first record of the result set or the last for [arg ORIGIN] values
[const current], [const first] and [const last] respectively.
[opt_def [cmd -strict]]
If specified, the command will raise an error if the offset or bookmark
seeks past the boundaries of the result set. By default, the cursor is
set to the position of the record at the boundary in such cases.
[list_end]

[call [cmd "EvtSession create"] [arg EVTSESSOBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a new TclOO object named [arg EVTSESSOBJ] encapsulating an event
log session. The command returns the fully qualified name of the
object.
[nl]
By default, the session is created for the local system
using the current process credentials.
The following options may be specified to instead connect to
a remote system or use different credentials.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -authtype [arg AUTHTYPE]]
Specifies the
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/winevt/ne-winevt-evt_rpc_login_flags "authentication method"]
to use for the connection.

[opt_def -domain [arg DOMAIN]]
Specifies the domain for the account specified by the [cmd -user]
option. This may be a Windows domain name or the name of the remote
computer for local accounts.

[opt_def -password [arg PASSWORD]]
Specifies the password corresponding to the account specified by the
[cmd -user] option. Must be in
[uri base.html#passwordprotection "encrypted form"].

[opt_def -system [arg SYSTEMNAME]]
Connects to the event log service on the system named [arg SYSTEMNAME]

[opt_def -user [arg USERNAME]]
Specifies the user account to use for the connection.
If omitted, the credentials of the current process are used.

[list_end]

[call [cmd "EvtSession new"] [opt [arg options]]]

Creates a new TclOO object with a unique name encapsulating an event log
session. Aside from generation of the object name, behaves identically
to [uri #evtsessioncreate [cmd "EvtSession create"]]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd archiveLogFile]" [arg LOGFILE] [opt "[cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]"]]
Adds localized strings to the events in the previously exported event log
file [arg LOGFILE]. Option [cmd -lcid] specifies the locale to use and defaults
to the process locale.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd clearChannel] [arg CHANNAME] [opt [arg options]]"]
Clears the specified channel. If option [cmd -backup] is specified, the channel
event records are backed up to the file whose path is given by the option value.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createChannelConfig]" [arg CHANCFGOBJ] [arg CHANNAME]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg CHANCFGOBJ]
of class [uri #evtchannelconfig [cmd EvtChannelConfig]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to the
configuration information for the channel [arg CHANNAME]. The returned object
is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createLogInfo]" [arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTLOGINFOOBJ] of class
[uri #evtloginfo [cmd EvtLogInfo]] and returns its fully qualified name.
The object wraps a handle to the configuration information for a channel or event
log file. The returned object is automatically destroyed when
[arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The channel or log file is specified through one of the following options.
The [cmd Application] channel is the default if no options are passed.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channel] [arg CHANNAME]]
The name of the channel to export. Must not be specified with [cmd -logfile].
[opt_def [cmd -logfile] [arg INFILE]]
The path to a event log file from which to read events.
Must not be specified with [cmd -channel].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createPublisher]" [arg EVTPUBOBJ] [arg PUBNAME] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTPUBOBJ] of class
[uri #evtpublisher [cmd EvtPublisher]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to the
metadata for the publisher named [arg PUBNAME]. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to the command.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
The LCID for accessing localized metadata. The default value of `0` uses
the thread's locale.
[opt_def [cmd -logarchive] [arg LOGARCHIVE]]
Path to an [uri #archivelogfile archived] log holding the publisher
metadata instead of the default metadata installed on the system.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createReader]" [arg EVTREADEROBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTREADEROBJ] of class
[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to
a EVT result set containing events from one or more channels or an
event log file. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to select events to be included
in the result set.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -channel] [arg CHANNAME]]
Specifies the event channel from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -file] option. Defaults to the [const Application]
channel if neither [cmd -channel] nor [cmd -logfile] is passed.
[opt_def [cmd -direction] [arg DIRECTION]]
If [arg DIRECTION] is [const forward] events are returned oldest
first. If [arg DIRECTION] is [const backward], events are returned
newest first.
[opt_def [cmd -logfile] [arg LOGFILE]]
Specifies the event file from which events are to be read. Cannot
be used with the [cmd -channel] option.
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If [cmd -ignorequeryerrors] is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERY]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERY] should be the empty string or [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/consuming-events "SDK documentation"].
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createSubscription]" [arg EVTSUBSCRIBEOBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTSUBSCRIBEOBJ] of class
[uri #evtsubscription [cmd EvtSubscription]]
and returns its fully qualified name. The object wraps a handle to an
EVT event subscription containing events from one or more channels. The
returned object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to select events to be included
in the result set.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -hbookmark] [arg HBOOKMARK]]
Handle to a bookmark. If specified, all events after the bookmark
that match the query are included in the subscription.
[opt_def [cmd -ignorequeryerrors]]
Normally, if retrieving a specific event results in an error, Windows
will fail the query and a Tcl exception will be raised.
If this option is specified, such events are simply skipped
and any further events are returned.
[opt_def [cmd -includeexisting]]
By default the command will only subscribe to new events. If this option
is specified, notification is sent for existing events as well.
[opt_def [cmd -query] [arg QUERY]]
Specifies the selection criteria for events that should be included in the
returned result set.
[arg QUERY] should be an empty string or [const *] (for all events, default),
an XPATH expression or a structured XML query. See
[uri https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/wes/consuming-events "SDK documentation"].
[arg [cmd -strict]]
If specified, an error is raised if the event referenced by passed bookmark
does not exist.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd createFormatter]" [arg EVTFMTOBJ] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates a TclOO object named [arg EVTFMTOBJ] of class
[uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]] and returns its fully qualified name.
The object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
[nl]
The following options may be passed to the command.
[list_begin opt]
[opt_def [cmd -lcid] [arg LCID]]
The LCID for accessing localized metadata. The default value of [const 0] uses
the thread's locale.
[opt_def [cmd -logarchive] [arg LOGARCHIVE]]
Path to an [uri #archivelogfile archived] log holding the publisher
metadata instead of the default metadata installed on the system.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd destroy]"]
Calling the [cmd destroy] method on a [cmd EvtSession] object will
close the wrapped Win32 EVT session handle. Any objects created, such as
[cmd EvtPublisher], [cmd EvtChannel] etc., through its factory
methods will also be destroyed.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd exportEvents]" [arg OUTFILE] [arg options]]
Exports events from a channel, logfile or a query to the file [arg OUTFILE]
which is overwritten. The following options may be specified.

[list_begin opt]
[opt_def -channel [arg CHANNAME]]
The name of the channel to export. Must not be specified with [cmd -logfile].
[opt_def -logfile [arg INFILE]]
The path to a event log file from which to read events.
Must not be specified with [cmd -channel].
[opt_def -query [arg QUERY]]
An XPath or structured XML query to select events to include in the export.
A value of [const *] or the empty string selects all events.
By default, all events are exported.
[opt_def -ignorequeryerrors [arg BOOL]]
If set to true, any errors in the query are ignored. Default is false.
[list_end]

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd handle]"]
Returns the wrapped Win32 EVT session handle.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd isLocal]"]
Return 1 if the session wrapped by the [arg SESSOBJ] object is the default local
session and 0 otherwise.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newChannelConfig]" [arg CHANNAME]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtchannelconfig [cmd EvtChannelConfig]].
The object wraps a handle to the
configuration information for the channel [arg CHANNAME]. The returned object
is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ] is destroyed.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newLogInfo]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtloginfo [cmd EvtLogInfo]]. The object wraps a handle to the
configuration information for a channel or event log file.
The returned object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ]
is destroyed. See [uri #createloginfo [cmd createLogInfo]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newPublisher]" [arg PUBNAME] [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtpublisher [cmd EvtPublisher]]. The object wraps a handle to the
metadata for the publisher named [arg PUBNAME]. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ] is destroyed. See
[uri #createpublisher [cmd createPublisher]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newReader]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtreader [cmd EvtReader]]. The object wraps a handle to the
a EVT result set containing events from one or more channels or an
event log file. The returned object is
automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSIONOBJ] is destroyed. See
[uri #createreader [cmd createReader]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newSubscription]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtsubscription [cmd EvtSubscription]]. The object wraps a handle to the
EVT event subscription containing events from one or more channels. The
returned object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
See [uri #createsubscription [cmd createSubscription]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd newFormatter]" [opt [arg options]]]
Creates and returns a uniquely named TclOO object of class
[uri #evtformatter [cmd EvtFormatter]].
The object is automatically destroyed when [arg EVTSESSOBJ] is destroyed.
See [uri #createformatter [cmd createFormatter]] for options.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd publishers]"]
Returns a list of the names of event publishers registered on
the system connected by the session.

[call "[arg EVTSESSOBJ] [cmd registeredChannels]"]
Returns a list of channel names registered on the system.

[list_end]


[keywords "event log"]

